  <h2>Warning signs, room by room</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What to look for</th><th>Likely source</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Ceilings</td><td>Stains, rings, sagging, peeling paint, drips at light fixtures</td><td>Bathroom above, roof, ice dam, attic condensation, HVAC drain line</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Walls</td><td>Bubbling paint, soft drywall, swollen baseboards, dark patches near the floor</td><td>Supply or drain line in the wall, shower valve, failed caulk or grout</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Floors</td><td>Cupped or buckled hardwood, soft spots, lifting vinyl, loose or cracked tile</td><td>Toilet wax ring, dishwasher, fridge line, slab or subfloor leak</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Under sinks</td><td>Warped cabinet base, stains, damp smell</td><td>Supply line, drain trap, faucet, garbage disposal</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Basement and crawl space</td><td>Efflorescence (white crust), damp walls, standing water, musty smell</td><td>Groundwater, gutters and grading, plumbing above, condensation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Outside</td><td>Constantly wet soil, unusually green patch, water at the foundation</td><td>Water service line, irrigation, downspouts</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>The water meter test</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Turn off all water use.</strong> Faucets, showers, the dishwasher, washing machine, ice maker, irrigation and any water softener regeneration.</li>
    <li><strong>Read the meter.</strong> Note the number, and watch the small leak indicator (often a triangle or dial) for movement.</li>
    <li><strong>Wait two hours</strong> without using any water.</li>
    <li><strong>Read it again.</strong> Any change means water is leaving the plumbing. If the meter moves with the main shutoff inside the house closed, the leak may be in the service line between the meter and the house.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>The toilet dye test</h2>
  <p>Toilets are among the most common sources of silent leaks. Put a few drops of food coloring in the tank, wait 10 to 15 minutes without flushing, and look in the bowl. Color in the bowl means the flapper is letting water through. That wastes water but usually doesn't damage the house; a leak at the base of the toilet, a loose supply line or a failed wax ring does.</p>

  <h2>Other clues</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The water bill.</strong> A higher bill with no change in use is a classic sign of a hidden leak.</li>
    <li><strong>Sounds.</strong> Running or hissing water when nothing is on, or a pump or well that cycles at night.</li>
    <li><strong>Hot spots on a slab floor.</strong> A warm patch on a concrete floor can point to a hot water line leaking under the slab.</li>
    <li><strong>Pests.</strong> Termites, carpenter ants and silverfish are drawn to damp wood.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Once you find it</h2>
  <ol class="steps">
    <li><strong>Stop the water</strong> at the fixture valve or the main.</li>
    <li><strong>Photograph and video</strong> the damage and the source before anything is removed.</li>
    <li><strong>Get a moisture inspection.</strong> Visible damage is usually the smallest part; a moisture map shows how far water spread inside walls and floors.</li>
    <li><strong>Call your insurer</strong> if the damage is significant. Coverage often turns on whether the leak was sudden or long-running, so give the facts and keep the failed part.</li>
  </ol>
